{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3151","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3151","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3151\",\"parent_revision\":\"ce87107b4377f4f4e70b202c41c8e38def9fb914\",\"root_revision\":\"644d298a2a0cd2e0424d3eedd18afaed326e72c118ef0524db1a675114f2a5fd\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA SAM task is not a file. It is an entry in the configured backend, read and written programmatically through the MCP operations or the CLI, addressed as `P{id}/T{id}`. It is never read or written by a file operation.\\n\\nThe plugin's own content still says otherwise in 138 places \u2014 92 in markdown, 46 in Python under `skills/implementation-manager/scripts/`. This has been corrected by instruction repeatedly over roughly six months and has not closed.\\n\\nObserved cause: correcting the prose does not work, because two authoritative sources actively regenerate it.\\n\\nFirst, the language is a live data contract, not a wording choice. `/dh:start-task` writes a session context file whose JSON key is literally `task_file_path` and whose value is a real filesystem path. The test suite exercises it across four files:\\n\\n    {\\\"task_file_path\\\": \\\"plan/P001.yaml\\\", \\\"task_id\\\": \\\"T1\\\"}\\n    {\\\"task_file_path\\\": \\\"/tmp/plan/P001-feature.yaml\\\", \\\"task_id\\\": \\\"T1\\\", ...}\\n\\nAny agent or hook reading that payload is correct to call the thing a task file \u2014 that is what the field is named and what it holds. A partial migration also stalled here: `tests_backlog/test_high_level_storage_boundaries.py` asserts `\\\"task_file_path\\\" not in source` for one module while the key remains the contract everywhere else, which pins the inconsistency rather than closing it.\\n\\nSecond, `AGENTS.md` instructs every agent to go and learn the file-based model. Its \\\"Required Reading by Task Type\\\" section routes task work to `docs/TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md` \u2014 29K, referenced from ten documents, with a second copy at `docs/sdlc-layers/layer-0/task-file-format.md`.\\n\\nSo the loop is: an agent reads `AGENTS.md`, is told to load `TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md`, learns tasks are files, and writes that into whatever it edits \u2014 in good faith, and consistently with the live JSON key. Prose is the loop's output, so each prose-scoped correction fixes the open file and leaves the sources intact.\\n\\nImpact: agents are taught a storage model the system does not use. Concrete consequences already observed \u2014 `skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` documents a parameter named `task_file_path`; `skills/backlog/references/item-schema.md` defines the `plan` field as \\\"relative path to SAM task file\\\"; `skills/execution/SKILL.md` has a workflow step \\\"Read Task File\\\"; `agents/code-reviewer.md` says \\\"Read the SAM task file using `mcp__plugin_dh_sam__sam_task`\\\", calling the right operation by the wrong name. A path-based handoff also silently produces an empty read in a worktree-isolated dispatch rather than an error, and on a remote backend there is no file to read at all.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the ordering that would close it appears to be structural-first \u2014 rename the context-file key and its payload to a plan/task address, retire `TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md` and remove it from `AGENTS.md` required reading, delete the layer-0 duplicate, and only then sweep the prose, which by that point has nothing regenerating it. Recorded as a hypothesis for grooming to validate, not as a requirement.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T12:23:52.026583Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: File-based language for SAM tasks persists because the context-file key and AGENTS.md required reading still teach it\\nDate: 2026-08-28\\nGoal: Remove task_file_path from the shared SAM task model and its regenerating documentation sources, then sweep remaining agent-facing prose.\\nConditions:\\n1. Current full list of code consumers reading task_file_path across all backends and hook scripts | Status: DERIVABLE\\n2. Exact test fixtures/assertions asserting task_file_path presence or absence that need updating | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. Whether any consumer outside plugins/development-harness depends on task_file_path | Status: DERIVABLE\\n4. Current location breakdown of the 57 remaining \\\"task file\\\" prose occurrences (post plan-side sweep) | Status: DERIVABLE\\n5. Whether beads.py's task_file_path fabrication (found this session, not named in original issue) affects the beads test suite | Status: DERIVABLE\\nAVAILABLE count: 1\\nDERIVABLE count: 4\\nMISSING count: 0\\n\\nScope sizing note: item self-describes as a recurring pattern (~6 months, repeated prose-only fixes that didn't hold), and this session's own verification already found scope beyond the original Impact Radius (beads.py fabricates task_file_path too, not named in the issue). Per analyze.md's escalation rule, sizing set to FULL (6 agents: impact-analyst, fact-checker, classifier, rtica-assessor, alignment-analyst, groomer) rather than the recurring-pattern default of STANDARD.\\n\\nWave 0 (technical-researcher) skipped: this is entirely internal repo code (no external library/API/ecosystem question), and the item's own Root Cause comments already carry exact file:line citations equivalent to Wave 0 output. Verified during this session: sibling plan-side problem already fixed via merged PRs #3159/#3161/#3137/#3156.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **file-based language for sam tasks persists because the context-file key and agents.md required reading still teach it** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 2026-08-22, while removing file-path language from start-task\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Second Regenerating Source Plans Not Just Tasks\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe description above covers SAM *tasks* being taught as files. The same loop runs independently for SAM *plans*, from sources it does not name \u2014 so closing the task side leaves this running.\\n\\n`agents/swarm-task-planner.md` creates plans through `sam_plan` (`create`/`append_task`/`finalize`) and never writes a plan file, yet its body still teaches a complete file-authoring model:\\n\\n- A \\\"Document Structure Policy\\\" prescribing a `PLAN.md` YAML frontmatter format whose keys \u2014 `description`, `version`, a `tasks:` list, `task_exports` \u2014 are fields on no SAM model, plus a `PLAN/` directory of per-priority part files for plans at or above 500 lines.\\n- A \\\"Large File Write Strategy\\\" section about a 25,000-character `Write` threshold for plan documents, task files, and `TASK/` exports.\\n- A \\\"Task Prompt Export Mode\\\" writing a `TASK/` directory.\\n- A \\\"Revision Management\\\" section instructing it to edit in place and never create `PLAN_v2.md`.\\n- T0/TN bookend task templates expressed as YAML blocks.\\n\\nThe regenerating source here is repo-level rather than plugin-level. `.claude/rules/large-file-write-strategy.md` loads on the relevant edits, names `swarm-task-planner` first in its scope list, and uses that agent's 500-line policy as its worked example:\\n\\n> **Canonical example**: The `swarm-task-planner` agent's 500-line progressive disclosure policy already implements Strategy A. Plans under 500 lines produce a single `PLAN.md`. Plans at or above 500 lines produce a `PLAN/` directory with `index.md` and per-priority part files.\\n\\nSo a rule that governs how any agent writes a large document teaches the plan-file model as its flagship illustration. An agent editing planner content reads that rule, sees `PLAN.md` presented as current practice, and preserves it \u2014 the same loop as `AGENTS.md` routing to `TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md`, one level up.\\n\\n### Plan-side inventory\\n\\nEstablished by reading the SAM backends and the callers, not by grepping prose:\\n\\n- `sam_plan` create/append_task/finalize persist through `sam_schema/writers/yaml_writer.py` into the backend's own plan directory. That is backend-internal storage, not a document an agent authors. No code path emits a `PLAN.md`, a `PLAN/` tree, or a `TASK/` export.\\n- `skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` Phase 4 is the planner's only caller. Its delegation prompt asks for the plan API and the returned plan ID, and names no file output.\\n- `task_exports` occurs outside the planner in exactly one place: `tests_sam/fixtures/nonstandard_frontmatter_tasks.md`, a negative-path fixture whose own notes state the canonical loader cannot parse that schema and that `tasks: []` in frontmatter raises \\\"No task entries found\\\". The planner was prescribing a format the loader rejects.\\n\\nContent still carrying the model, beyond the planner itself:\\n\\n- `skills/context-integration/SKILL.md` \u2014 instructs updating a Contextualization Status checklist \\\"at the bottom of PLAN.md\\\". The live equivalent is the plan's `context` field.\\n- `skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md` \u2014 rules phrased against `PLAN.md` and `DISCOVERY.md` by name. The underlying rule (inline context rather than cross-reference) is live; the artefact names are not.\\n- `agents/t0-baseline-capture.md` \u2014 a \\\"Read Plan File\\\" step using a file read on a passed plan path. `agents/tn-verification-gate.md` reaches the same data through `artifact_read`. The two bookend agents disagree with each other about how the plan is reached, which is a live inconsistency rather than only stale wording.\\n- `agents/feature-researcher.md` \u2014 carries the full 25K Write strategy. This agent does still write a feature-context document, so whether the strategy applies to it must be checked before removal rather than assumed dead.\\n- `agents/codebase-analyzer.md` \u2014 migrated in substance (it states section appends have no single-call size limit) but retains a 25K-per-call figure.\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/agents/python-cli-design-spec.md` and its duplicate at `plugins/python3-development/agents/python-cli-design-spec.md` \u2014 both express the write strategy against `sam_create` and `sam_update`, tool names absent from the current SAM surface.\\n- `plugins/python3-development/planning/sse-gap-analysis.md` \u2014 states the planner produces `PLAN.md` and `TASK/` files.\\n\\nNote the last three are outside `development-harness`, so the plan-file model has propagated across plugin boundaries and one instance is duplicated between two plugins.\\n\\nTwo observations recorded rather than asserted:\\n\\n- Whether the 25K write strategy still has any reachable target for these agents after the API migration is not established here. If plans and artifacts are backend records, the threshold may no longer apply to the agents the rule names, which would make the rule's scope list stale rather than merely its example.\\n- `tests_sam/test_cli_migrate_fallback.py` and `tests_sam/fixtures/nonstandard_frontmatter_tasks.md` reference the file format. Migration paths legitimately need to read the old format, so these are not necessarily drift \u2014 the distinction between reading a legacy format and teaching it as current is what needs checking.\\n\\nOrdering consequence for the hypothesis above: the plan-side sources are `.claude/rules/large-file-write-strategy.md` and the planner's own body, and they are independent of the context-file key and `TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md`. Sweeping prose on either side alone leaves the other regenerating.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"80f52588088a1a25eb1addb34e06ff2b9fc40425c60db78dd09b91dbac020a01\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRP4RUg\"}"}